WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.462
Two common filtering patterns are BETWEEN for ranges
and IN for a

00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:08.238
list of allowed values BETWEEN is convenient when
you want values inside

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:13.012
an interval For example age BETWEEN thirty AND sixty
includes age thirty

00:00:13.162 --> 00:00:17.612
age sixty and everything between them It is
important to remember that

00:00:17.638 --> 00:00:22.162
the endpoints are included IN is helpful when one
column can match

00:00:22.175 --> 00:00:26.738
any value from a small list For example status IN
open and

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:30.050
pending is often easier to read than writing status
equals open OR

00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:35.175
status equals pending The example on this slide
combines a category condition

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.488
with a range We want female patients whose age is
between thirty

00:00:39.512 --> 00:00:43.912
and sixty The two conditions are connected with AND
so both must

00:00:43.925 --> 00:00:48.275
be true Then ORDER BY age arranges the qualifying
rows from lower

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.425
age to higher age because ascending order is the
default Run the

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:58.125
query and inspect the rows Then try changing the
range For example

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.588
change thirty to forty and see whether the result
becomes smaller You

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.075
could also replace the gender condition with a
different value and compare

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.462
the output The important lesson is that SQL gives us
several readable

00:01:10.475 --> 00:01:15.050
ways to express filtering logic Choose the pattern
that makes the condition

00:01:15.075 --> 00:01:16.375
easiest to understand
